SELECT i.slug,'Government',
'Texas Military Installations: $148.8 Billion Economic Impact, 628,884 Jobs',
'Texas Comptroller data based on 2025 military activity estimates $148.8 billion in economic output and more than 628,000 direct and indirect jobs; the original July article is transparently reconstructed.',
NULL::text,'Keep TX Red Newsroom','Texas Comptroller of Public Accounts','https://comptroller.texas.gov/about/media-center/news/20260702-texas-comptroller-releases-military-installation-economic-impact-analysis-1782849392188',
NULL::text,NULL::text,NULL::text,NULL::text,
'2026-07-10T18:00:00Z'::timestamptz,'news','{"updated":"2026-10-14T19:58:00Z","intro":["An economic-impact study released by the Texas Comptroller of Public Accounts on July 2, 2026 estimated that the state''s military installations generated approximately $148.8 billion in annual economic output and supported 628,884 direct and indirect jobs. Governor Greg Abbott highlighted the findings again on July 10, describing military communities as both national-security assets and major Texas employers.","The numbers are estimates from an analysis of 2025 economic activity rather than a new 2026 defense appropriation or a statement that the state government received $148.8 billion in tax revenue. This October reconstruction restores a July 10 Keep TX Red news URL whose original article copy is missing; it is based on the comptroller''s report, statewide data and the governor''s announcement."],"sections":[{"heading":"The statewide findings and what they measure","paragraphs":["The comptroller''s July 2 release attributed almost $148.8 billion of economic output to Texas military installations and related activity. It listed approximately $91.4 billion in state gross domestic product and $43.6 billion in disposable personal income under the methodology described by the agency. Those are different economic measures and should not be added together as if they were three separate pots of public funds.","The report''s employment estimate includes direct military- and civilian-associated positions as well as additional jobs supported elsewhere in Texas when workers, contractors and businesses spend money. The governor''s July 10 announcement cited 628,884 total jobs. Analysts and readers should distinguish direct staffing from modeled indirect employment when comparing the report against Department of Defense headcounts or private business employment data."]},{"heading":"What the jobs figure does and does not prove","paragraphs":["Military facilities employ active-duty personnel, civilian employees, reservists, National Guard members and contractors. Their operations also support suppliers, maintenance firms, health services, retail businesses and other local activities. These spillovers help explain why the report''s total employment estimate exceeds the number of personnel directly assigned to bases.","The governor''s summary reported 212,524 direct jobs; the comptroller''s July 2 press release identified 212,541. These closely related but nonidentical published figures should not be silently merged into an invented exact count. For consistent statewide comparison, this reconstruction uses the shared total employment estimate of 628,884 and directs readers to the report''s own line-by-line tables for the directly employed population."]},{"heading":"Why Texas bases matter to regional communities","paragraphs":["The impact is uneven across the state. Fort Bliss near El Paso, the Joint Base San Antonio complex, Fort Hood, several Air Force bases and Navy installations support distinct missions and different regional labor markets. Some are concentrated in major cities, while others are anchors for communities where defense activity accounts for an unusually large share of employment.","Installation-related economic activity affects local housing demand, schools, health facilities, roads, small businesses and contractor networks. An estimate of statewide output does not mean every community benefits equally or that all jobs are permanently guaranteed. Changes in missions, procurement budgets, deployments and federal decisions can shift local effects over time."]},{"heading":"The analysis was published in stages","paragraphs":["The comptroller released the economic-impact analysis on July 2, 2026 with input from the Texas Military Preparedness Commission. The governor then highlighted an approximately $148.8 billion economic contribution on July 10. Those were connected publications concerning the same study, not two separate sets of billions in military spending.","The comptroller''s site offers a statewide snapshot and installation-specific pages. Readers who want to know how a nearby installation contributed to direct jobs, wider economic output or disposable income should use those specific tables, keeping the 2025 study year and underlying assumptions attached to any quoted figure."]},{"heading":"How the military preparedness commission fits in","paragraphs":["The Texas Military Preparedness Commission advises state leaders on ways to support military communities, protect installation missions and prepare for potential federal base realignment or closure decisions. The commission works with communities on infrastructure, mission readiness and resilience. Its role in the economic study helps explain why the state tracks economic dependence on bases alongside national-security considerations.","The report can inform planning discussions but should not by itself be treated as proof that a particular grant, tax incentive or construction project produced a measured return. Policy evaluation would require comparing the relevant spending to separately documented changes in jobs, readiness, resilience or local economic performance."]},{"heading":"What the $148.8 billion estimate is not","paragraphs":["The amount is not the state''s annual military payroll, a single federal appropriation, a Texas tax receipt, or a cash transfer to military families. The study''s concept of economic output captures the value of related activity under a model. Confusing that with gross domestic product can overstate the meaning of the headline number and distort comparisons with the state budget.","Similarly, total jobs include estimated secondary effects rather than simply counting people in uniform. The reported figures are useful for understanding the economic footprint of installations, but both the methodology and the date of the underlying study should accompany any citation."]},{"heading":"Original URL restoration and verification sources","paragraphs":["Keep TX Red previously carried this topic under a July 10, 2026 news address. The original body text was not found in the current database or repository after earlier newsroom changes. The article was reconstructed October 14 from the official comptroller analysis, installation tables and governor''s follow-up release. It preserves the historic URL but not the lost reporter''s wording.","Readers can follow the sources below to inspect the underlying statewide and installation-level methodology. No photograph of a particular base is presented as documenting every installation in the economic study, and the article does not infer new federal defense funding from the modeled economic figures."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How much did Texas military installations contribute according to the 2026 report?","a":"The study estimated roughly $148.8 billion in economic output based on 2025 activity; the figure is not state tax revenue or a new appropriation."},{"q":"How many jobs were supported by military installations?","a":"The governor and comptroller cited approximately 628,884 direct and indirect jobs statewide, based on the same 2025 economic-impact analysis."},{"q":"Why do direct-jobs numbers differ slightly between announcements?","a":"The comptroller''s July 2 release and the governor''s July 10 summary list slightly different direct-job counts. Readers should use the underlying report and clearly state which edition or table they are quoting."}],"sources":[{"label":"Texas Comptroller — July 2 economic impact release","url":"https://comptroller.texas.gov/about/media-center/news/20260702-texas-comptroller-releases-military-installation-economic-impact-analysis-1782849392188"},{"label":"Governor Greg Abbott — July 10 military economic impact announcement","url":"https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-announces-148.8-billion-economic-impact-of-military-installations-in-texas"},{"label":"Texas Comptroller — statewide report and installation profiles","url":"https://comptroller.texas.gov/economy/economic-data/military/"},{"label":"Texas Comptroller — installation-by-installation statewide snapshot","url":"https://comptroller.texas.gov/economy/economic-data/military/2025/statewide-snapshot.php"}],"keyTakeaways":["An analysis of 2025 Texas military activity estimated nearly $148.8 billion in statewide economic output.","The study associated 628,884 direct and indirect Texas jobs with installations and related activity.","These modeled figures describe an economic footprint, not a new appropriation or state tax revenue."]}'::jsonb,
